From {{inh|en|enm|awaken}} and {{m|enm|awakien}}, from {{inh|en|ang|āwacan}} and {{m|ang|āwacian}}. {{surf|+af|en|a-|wake}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb|~s|awaking|awoke,~d<l:rare>|awoken,~d<l:rare>,awoke<l:rare>,~n<l:rare>}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To become [[conscious]] after having [[sleep|slept]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1859|title=[[w:Rubaiyat of Omar Khayyam|The Rub&aacute;iy&aacute;t of Omar Khayy&aacute;m: The Astronomer-Poet of Persia]]|author=w:Edward Fitzgerald|page=1|text='''Awake'''! for Morning in the Bowl of Night,<br>Has flung the Stone that puts the Stars to Flight:<br>And Lo! the Hunter of the East has caught<br>The Sult&aacute;n's Turret in a Noose of light.}}
#: {{syn|en|awaken|wake up|Thesaurus:wake}}
#: {{ant|en|fall asleep|Thesaurus:fall asleep}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To cause (somebody) to stop [[sleep|sleeping]].
#: {{syn|en|bring round|cry|wake up|Thesaurus:awaken}}
#: {{ant|en|put to sleep}}
#* {{RQ:Mlry MrtDrthr|19.1|2|XVII|primum|passage=Thenne she called the heremyte syre Vlfyn I am a gentylwoman that wold speke with the knyght whiche is with yow / Thenne the good man '''awaked''' Galahad / & badde hym aryse and speke with a gentylwoman that semeth hath grete nede of yow / Thenne Galahad wente to her & asked her what she wold}}
<!--Prior quote:Than the good man '''awaked''' Sir Galahad and bade him aryse-->
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1665|author=w:Robert Hooke|title=w:Micrographia
|passage=[This ant] I ſuffered to lye above an hour in the Spirit; and after I had taken it out, and put its body and legs into a natural poſture, remained moveleſs about an hour; but then , upon a ſudden, as if it had been '''awaken''' out of a drunken ſleep, it ſuddenly reviv'd and ran away...}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To make aware of something.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[excite]] or to [[stir up]] something [[latent]].
# {{lb|en|transitive|figurative}} To rouse from a state of [[inaction]] or [[dormancy]].
# {{lb|en|intransitive|figurative}} To come out of a state of [[inaction]] or [[dormancy]].
#* '''1867-1879''', {{w|Edward Augustus Freeman}}, ''The History of the Norman Conquest of England''
#*: The national spirit again '''awoke'''.
#* {{RQ:KJV|1 Corinthians|15|34||'''Awake''' to righteousness, and sin not.}}
# {{used in phrasal verbs|lang=en|to awake to|t=to become awake of something and its possible effects or results.}}
#: {{ux|en|It took her some time '''to awake to''' the dangers of her situation.}}
# {{used in phrasal verbs|lang=en|to awake to|t=to be roused from one's sleep, due to a noise or other stimulus.}}
#: {{ux|en|this morning, I '''awoke to''' the sound of my neighbor's barking dog.}}

=====Usage notes=====
For many speakers, this verb is commonly conflated with {{m|en|awaken}} (and, in such cases, lends some conjugational elements to it). See the usage notes at {{m|en|awaken}} for more details.

