[[File:Euclid statue, Oxford University Museum of Natural History, UK - 20080315.jpg|thumb|A statue honouring the Greek [[mathematician]] {{w|Euclid}} (''[[floruit|fl.]]'' 300 [[BCE|{{smallcaps|b.c.e.}}]]) at the {{w|Oxford University Museum of Natural History}} in [[Oxford]], [[Oxfordshire]], [[England]], UK]]
[[File:Parallel postulate en.svg|thumb|One of Euclid’s [[distinctive]] [[postulate]]s, or axioms, is the [[parallel postulate]], which states that if a [[line segment]] [[intersect]]s two straight lines forming two [[interior angle]]s ''(α and β in the diagram above)'' on the same side that sum to less than two [[right angle]]s, then the two lines, if extended indefinitely, meet on that side on which the angles sum to less than two right angles]]
