# The act of [[assert]]ing; [[positive]] [[declaration]] or [[averment]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1869|author={{w|Alfred Russel Wallace}}|title={{w|The Malay Archipelago}}|publisher=Macmillan and Co.|year_published=|volume=II|location=London|page=247|passage="Do tell us the real name of your country, and then when you are gone we shall know how to talk about you." To this luminous argument and remonstrance I could oppose nothing but '''assertion''', and the whole party remained firmly convinced that I was for some reason or other deceiving them.}}
# Something which is asserted; a declaration; a statement asserted.
#: {{syn|en|contention}}
#: {{ux|en|You're a man of strong '''assertions'''!}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=26 January 2007|author=Ruth M. J. Byrne|publisher=w:MIT Press|title=The Rational Imagination: How People Create Alternatives to Reality|isbn=9780262261845|url=https://books.google.co.nz/books?id=JHDCzU6UzgEC|page=140|passage=Suppose you are given the semifactual '''assertion''', "even if Nora had liked mathematics then she would have became{{sic}} a scientist" and then you find out that Nora did in fact become a scientist.}}
# A statement or declaration which lacks support or evidence.
#: {{ux|en|That's just a bare '''assertion'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2022 August 24|author=Philip Haigh|title=Network News: Union slams Avanti West Coast: 'lie' as services slashed|journal=RAIL|issue=964|page=6|text=Drivers' union ASLEF bluntly rebuffed the claim of unofficial action, calling it a lie. And Avanti West Coast was unable to provide any proof for its '''assertion''', when questioned by ''RAIL''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Jennifer Hansler; Natasha Bertrand; Kylie Atwood; Zachary Cohen|title=Trump has claimed Iran is building missiles that could soon hit the US. Sources say that’s not backed up by US intelligence.|magazine=w:CNN|url=https://www.cnn.com/2026/02/27/politics/iran-missiles-trump-intelligence-nuclear|date=2026-02-27|passage=However, that '''assertion''' is not backed up by US intelligence, sources told CNN. {{...}} That claim came despite the Trump administration’s repeated '''assertions''' that the US had “obliterated” Iran’s nuclear program in military strikes last year.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260303021412/https://www.cnn.com/2026/02/27/politics/iran-missiles-trump-intelligence-nuclear}}
# {{U|maintenance}}; [[vindication]].
#: {{co|en|the '''assertion''' of one's rights or prerogatives}}
# {{lb|en|programming}} A [[statement]] in a [[program]] asserting a [[condition]] expected to be true at a particular point, used in [[debug]]ging.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2006|author=Srikanth Vijayaraghavan; Meyyappan Ramanathan|title=A Practical Guide for SystemVerilog Assertions|page=284
|passage=The user should be absolutely confident that the error issued is a real design error. In other words, a user should be confident that his '''assertion''' code is correct and that the '''assertion''' failure is not a false condition.}}
# {{lb|en|finance}} The set of information that the statement preparer is providing in a financial statement audit.

