===Etymology 3===
From {{inh|en|enm|associat|alt=associat(e)||associated, allied}} (the verb ''*associaten'' is not found in {{w|Middle English}} writings and only attested at a later period), see {{af|en|-ate|id1=verb|pos1=verb-forming suffix}} and [[# Etymology 1|Etymology 1]] for more. {{doublet|en|associe}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To join in or form a [[league]], [[union]], or [[association]].
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To spend time [[social]]ly; keep [[company]].
#: {{syn|en|surround|forgather|q2=formal}}
#: {{ux|en|She '''associates''' with her coworkers on weekends.}}
#: {{ux|en|Before the race he '''associate'''d only with other skiers.}}
#* {{RQ:Birmingham Gossamer|chapter=I|passage=As a political system democracy seems to me extraordinarily foolish,{{nb...}}. My servant is, so far as I am concerned, welcome to as many votes as he can get.{{...}}I do not suppose that it matters much in reality whether laws are made by dukes or cornerboys, but I like, as far as possible, to '''associate''' with gentlemen in private life.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|with ''with''}} To [[join]] as a [[partner]], [[ally]], or [[friend]].
#: {{ux|en|He '''associated''' his name with many environmental causes.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[connect]] or join together; [[combine]].
#: {{syn|en|attach|join|put together|unite|Thesaurus:join}}
#: {{ux|en|particles of gold '''associated''' with other substances}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To connect [[evidentially]], or in the [[mind]] or [[imagination]].
#* '''1819''' September 21, {{w|John Keats}}, letter to {{w|John Hamilton Reynolds}}:
#*: I always somehow '''associate''' Chatterton with autumn.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1848|author={{w|Thomas Babington Macaulay, 1st Baron Macaulay|Thomas Babington Macaulay}}|title=The History of England from the Accession of James II|url=https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/2613|passage=He succeeded in '''associating''' his name inseparably with some names which will last as long as our language.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1951|month=August|author=P. W. Gentry|title=Cliff Railways|journal=Railway Magazine|page=514|text=A branch of rail transport that seems to been rather neglected by historians is that concerned with cliff railways, of which a fair number exist in Great Britain. This is probably because these lines are overshadowed by the more spectacular funicular railways in Switzerland and other mountainous regions; perhaps, too, because of the general habit of referring to them as "cliff lifts," which tends to '''associate''' them with the vertical indoor type.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1960|month=December|title=The Glasgow Suburban Electrification is opened|journal=Trains Illustrated|page=714|text=The economics of rebuilding all the stations covered by the electrification would be prohibitive, but to help bring home to the Glasgow public that their North Clyde suburban service has been transformed, not merely re-equipped with new trains, stations have at least been '''associated''' psychologically with the rolling stock by a common colour scheme.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2013|month=July-August|author=[http://www.americanscientist.org/authors/detail/philip-j-bushnell Philip J. Bushnell]|title=[http://www.americanscientist.org/issues/feature/2013/4/solvents-ethanol-car-crashes-amp-tolerance Solvents, Ethanol, Car Crashes & Tolerance]|magazine=w:American Scientist|passage=Surprisingly, this analysis revealed that acute exposure to solvent vapors at concentrations below those '''associated''' with long-term effects appears to increase the risk of a fatal automobile accident.}}
# {{lb|en|reflexive|in deliberative bodies}} To [[endorse]].
#: {{ux|en|She refused to '''associate''' herself with the petition.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=Congressional Record|volume=145|chapter=Pt. 14|month=August 4-5|year=1999|page=19343|author=Congress|passage=Mr. President, I rise to '''associate''' myself with the remarks of my senior Senator from Louisiana who has led this fight successfully for many years}}
# {{lb|en|mathematics}} To be [[associative]].
# {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete}} To [[accompany]]; to be in the company of.
#: {{syn|en|[[travel]] (U.S.)}}
